From: Rabs9 <241389662+Rabs9@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/sunxi: implement DIRTYFB so damage actually reaches the panel

sunxi_drm_gem_fb_funcs never set a .dirty callback, so the DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DIRTYFB
ioctl returned -ENOSYS. The X modesetting driver probes with exactly that ioctl
(dispatch_dirty() in hw/xfree86/drivers/modesetting/driver.c) and, on -EINVAL or
-ENOSYS, permanently disables damage tracking:

    ret = dispatch_dirty_region(scrn, crtc, pixmap, ms->damage, fb_id);
    if (ret == -EINVAL || ret == -ENOSYS) {
        ms->dirty_enabled = FALSE;
        ...
        xf86DrvMsg(scrn->scrnIndex, X_INFO,
                   "Disabling kernel dirty updates, not required.\n");

That log line is not X deciding the feature is unnecessary - it is X being told
the kernel cannot do it. With damage tracking off, dirtied regions are never
pushed to the display engine, so anything drawn once stays on screen forever:
cursor trails that never clear, orphaned window content, stale rectangles.

Everything downstream was compensating for this. The vendor stack shipped picom
with --no-use-damage plus an "xheartbeat" ticker whose only job was to
manufacture 30Hz damage so the compositor would repaint the entire screen and
force a flush. That cost ~60% of a CPU core on a 1024x600 panel. With this
patch, glamor runs on the PowerVR with no compositor at all and Xorg idles at
about 2%.

drm_atomic_helper_dirtyfb() is the standard helper for atomic drivers; rockchip
(rockchip_drm_fb.c), vmwgfx and amdgpu use it the same way.

Signed-off-by: Rabs9 <241389662+Rabs9@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/drm/sunxi_drm_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/drm/sunxi_drm_drv.c
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 #include <drm/drm_gem_dma_helper.h>
 #endif
 #include <drm/drm_gem_framebuffer_helper.h>
+#include <drm/drm_damage_helper.h>
 #include <drm/drm_print.h>
 #include <drm/drm_probe_helper.h>
 #include <drm/drm_vblank.h>
@@ -109,6 +110,13 @@
 static const struct drm_framebuffer_funcs sunxi_drm_gem_fb_funcs = {
 	.destroy = sunxi_drm_gem_fb_destroy,
 	.create_handle = drm_gem_fb_create_handle,
+	/* Without a .dirty callback the DIRTYFB ioctl returns -ENOSYS, and the
+	 * X modesetting driver responds by disabling damage tracking entirely
+	 * ("Disabling kernel dirty updates, not required"). Damaged regions
+	 * then never reach the panel: cursor trails, stale rectangles and
+	 * orphaned window content persist until something repaints the whole
+	 * screen. Same helper rockchip and friends use. */
+	.dirty = drm_atomic_helper_dirtyfb,
 };
 
 struct drm_framebuffer *
